    "description": "Billstream generates customer invoices that are delivered through three channels: an HTML portal page, a printable PDF, and a plain-text email. All three contain the same customer, line items, discounts, tax summary, due date, and payment instructions, but their visual conventions and output rules differ. The current implementation has three separate generation paths, so adding a discount field or changing invoice wording requires coordinated edits and the email version sometimes disagrees with the portal version. Domain code already owns monetary calculations, but it returns channel-specific formatting structures. The team wants the invoice's content and structure to be represented independently of any output format, with format-specific components responsible for layout, typography, escaping, pagination, and transport packaging. Changes to business content should propagate to every channel, while a visual redesign of the portal or email must not require rewriting invoice calculations. A machine-readable invoice API also exists, but it exposes typed fields rather than reproducing the customer-facing invoice layout, so it should not be forced into the same presentation pipeline.",
    "decision_statement": "Should invoice structure be produced independently and then rendered for HTML, PDF, and email, or should each channel duplicate its own invoice-building and formatting logic?",
    "notes": "two-step-view fits building one logical invoice page before rendering HTML, PDF, and email; multiple-views is the per-channel-view alternative, with view-helper and template-view supporting formatting and markup."
  },
